# Code Review Checklist

Work through this list whenever you change code or the user asks you to review
it. Cover every **touched file** and its closely related call sites.

## Review the changed code for

- **Antipatterns** — logic in the wrong layer, scattered env checks, rescue-all error handling, bypassing validations, magic strings/numbers
- **Code smells** — long methods, deep nesting, feature envy, shotgun surgery, dead code
- **Unnecessary duplication** — extract only when duplication is real and repeated; prefer existing project patterns over new abstractions
- **Unclear naming** — methods, variables, and specs should state intent; rename when names mislead
- **Missing tests** — flag behavior that is non-trivial, conditional, or easy to regress (test rules live in `code-change-standards`)

## Refresh comments and the README (every review)

- Read the comments in each touched file. Fix or delete any that are now stale,
  misleading, or describe code that no longer exists.
- Update the **README** whenever a change affects setup, dependencies, commands,
  configuration, or documented behavior.
- Comments must explain intent or constraints — never narrate what the code does.

<example>
A gem is removed, leaving `# Use CoffeeScript for .coffee assets and views`
above unrelated code. The reviewer deletes the now-false comment.
</example>

## Then act on what you find

Fix the issues before finishing. Do not defer quality problems to a follow-up
unless the user explicitly scoped the task narrower.
